# White-paper content, part C: Methodology, Discussion, Conclusions, References
# All numbers verified against srs_tank_waste.db queries run 2026-10-16 (post FTF re-parse fix).

METHOD = [
    {"t": "h1", "text": "3. Methodology"},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "3.1 Data sources"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "All data are from public documents and public APIs; no proprietary or WCS-internal data were used. The SRS side draws on twelve primary documents. Farm-level current inventories come from the SRS Liquid Waste System Plan (LWSP) Revision 24 (SRMC-LWP-2023-00001), which transmits the Waste Characterization System (WCS) Curie and Volume Inventory Report dated September 30, 2025, with historical snapshots from Revision 22 (data as of December 31, 2018) and a 2016 fact-sheet composite. Per-tank closure inventories come from the performance-assessment chain: the H-Area Tank Farm Performance Assessment Rev 1 (SRR-CWDA-2010-00128, Tables 3.4-9 and 3.4-10, the original per-tank basis), the Tank 16 Special Analysis for the HTF PA (SRR-CWDA-2014-00106, Tables 6.2-2 through 6.2-5, the currently controlling assigned basis for all 29 HTF tanks), the Tanks 5 and 6 Special Analysis for the FTF PA Rev 1 (SRR-CWDA-2012-00106, Tables 6.1-1 and 6.1-2, the revised basis for all 22 FTF tanks), and the Tanks 18 and 19 Special Analysis plus Closure Module (SRR-CWDA-2010-00106 and SRR-CWDA-2010-00003) for measured actual residuals. Section 3116 basis documents for both farms (DOE/SRS-WD-2013-001 draft and DOE/SRS-WD-2012-001) provide the regulatory context. Measured current-waste composition comes from seven SRNL qualification reports (SRNL-STI-2024-00492, -2024-00240, -2024-00436, -2025-00489, -2025-00012, OSTI 2473257, and SRNL-STI-2024-00506) covering Sludge Batch 10 and 11, SWPF Salt Batches 8 through 14, and PRFT batches."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The Hanford side is the Best-Basis Inventory as served by the PNNL PHOENIX TankFarmService API (BBIWaste and BBIVolume endpoints), pulled per tank for all 177 tanks. Each tank carries its own effective date (the date its BBI snapshot was computed), ranging from 2015-10-01 to 2026-05-01 across the fleet, with the bulk of tanks on 2022 through 2026 snapshots; 139 tanks are decayed to 2022-01-01 and 38 tanks, mostly older single-shell tanks with legacy estimates, to 2015-07-01. The BBI reports 50 radionuclides in curies and 140 chemicals in kilograms per tank, split by waste phase, with data-quality qualifiers (5,558 of the 46,600 rows carry a less-than qualifier). Upstream of PHOENIX, the BBI is maintained in TWINS, and its official tabulation is the Waste Information Data System (WIDS) report HNF-EP-0182."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "3.2 Parsing and assembly pipeline"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The pipeline is fully scripted and reproducible from the repository. PDF text extraction (pdftotext layout-preserving mode) feeds hand-verified table parsers for each document's table format. The PA and SA tables are wide-format matrices (nuclide or chemical rows, tank columns) and are parsed positionally: each row is split into exactly as many value cells as the table has tank columns, with not-determined (ND) and not-established (NE) tokens retained as cells rather than skipped, because skipping them shifts every subsequent value one column to the left. The SRNL batch reports use several distinct table layouts (element tables, anion tables, radionuclide tables with dual units, waste-acceptance-criteria tables); a dedicated parser handles each layout and records the unit, matrix (slurry, supernate, filtrate, dried solids), and report provenance on every row. Hanford data required no parsing: the API returns structured JSON that loads directly into the database."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Every row in the database carries a dataset label, a basis description, and a source pointer into a document registry, so that current, assigned-closure, PA-original, annulus, and measured-actual bases can be selected or excluded explicitly in any query. The database schema mirrors the BBI's tank, analyte, phase, value, unit structure so cross-site queries stay symmetric."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "3.3 Normalization decisions"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Four decisions matter for anyone reusing the data. First, volumes: Hanford BBI volume rows include drained-void and retained-gas buckets (9,539 kL across 39 rows at the latest snapshots) that are not physical waste; these are excluded from all volume totals here, which is why the site total is 53.0 Mgal rather than the 55.5 Mgal a naive sum gives. Second, aggregates: the BBI includes combined-analyte rows (239/240Pu, 243/244Cm, 144Ce/Pr) and gross measures (TotalAlpha); these are excluded from radionuclide sums to avoid double counting, which changes the Hanford activity total by less than 0.05 percent. Third, daughter products: both sites count Y-90 with Sr-90 and Ba-137m with Cs-137; the comparisons in this paper keep those pairs together and treat them as single inventory families. Fourth, per-tank snapshots: Hanford BBI rows are summed per tank on each tank's own latest effective date; no single site-wide snapshot date exists, and pretending otherwise would drop tanks whose latest revision post-dates the chosen date."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "3.4 The assigned-closure basis and its conservatisms"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The SRS assigned-closure values deserve their own description because they are the SRS public data most similar to a per-tank inventory, and they are conservative in specific, documented ways. The HTF assignment (Tank 16 SA Table 6.2-2) groups the 29 tanks into waste-type groups and assigns every tank in a group the maximum inventory estimated for any tank in that group: all seven Type I and II tanks (9 through 15, minus 16) receive identical 49.6 kCi totals, and the 16 Type III/IIIA tanks (29 through 51, minus 32 and 36) all receive identical values. This is deliberate: performance assessment needs a defensible upper bound for tanks that will not be individually characterized at closure. The FTF revision (Tanks 5 and 6 SA Table 6.1-1) instead carries per-tank projected values for operating tanks and measured actuals for the closed tanks. The assignment basis decays all values to 2032, the assumed closure horizon. For the two tanks where both bases exist, the agreement is exact: Tanks 18 and 19 carry 25.1 and 8.2 kCi on both the assigned and measured-actual bases. Chemical assignments mirror the radionuclide tables with the same grouping."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "One parsing defect found and fixed during this work illustrates why the validation discipline matters. In the original parse of FTF Table 6.1-1, ND tokens in a row were skipped rather than counted as cells, shifting values leftward for tanks 17 through 20; on the Zr-93 row the parser then consumed a value from the table's total row, assigning 290 kCi of Zr-93 to Tanks 19 and 20 and inflating those tanks' totals by factors of 36 and 2,400 respectively, and the all-51-tank closure total from 5.80 to 6.38 MCi. The re-parse treats ND and NE as real cells and validates every tank's nuclide sum against the source table's own Total Curies by Tank row (22 of 22 tanks match within the table's two-significant-figure rounding). The corrected values reproduce the measured actuals for Tanks 18 and 19 exactly, which the corrupted values did not. All numbers in this paper are computed from the corrected dataset."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "3.5 Validation summary"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Validation rests on internal consistency checks and cross-document agreement. Internal: per-tank nuclide sums reproduce each source table's printed totals for all 22 FTF tanks (within two-significant-figure rounding) and the HTF grouped assignments are internally consistent with the SA's reported farm totals (HTF 3.11 MCi, FTF 2.70 MCi). Cross-document: Tanks 18 and 19 residuals agree between the Special Analysis and the Closure Module; Tank 16 actual residuals (18.8 kCi) match between its SA Tables 5.0-1 and the assigned table; HTF assigned values match the earlier PA Rev 1 Table 3.4-9 for tanks whose grouping did not change. Hanford totals were checked against the published BBI summary: the 122.5 MCi site total computed here from per-tank nuclide sums is consistent with the 122.6 MCi reported in the latest HNF-EP-0182 revision, the small difference being aggregate-row treatment and per-tank snapshot dates."},
]

DISCUSSION = [
    {"t": "h1", "text": "4. Discussion"},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "4.1 Basis asymmetry and what it permits"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The central limitation of any SRS-Hanford comparison is that the public data models are not the same object. Hanford's BBI is a current inventory: what is in each tank now, by phase, maintained for operations. The SRS per-tank public data are closure residuals: what regulators assume will remain after the tanks are emptied and cleaned. Comparing them directly mixes a snapshot with a forecast, and the direction of the resulting error is predictable: it always understates SRS. The SRS current farm total is 191 MCi; the assigned-closure total is 5.80 MCi, a 33-fold difference. Any figure that mixes the two will be wrong by an order of magnitude or more."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The comparison is still tractable if each question is mapped to the basis that can answer it. Current-versus-current questions (how much waste is left, how hot is it per gallon, which phases hold the activity) use the SRS farm totals against Hanford BBI sums, and accept that SRS answers are farm-level only. Closure-versus-closure questions (what will remain after cleanup) use the SRS assigned residuals against a Hanford residual estimate, though Hanford publishes no per-tank closure residual, so this direction of comparison is actually the harder one and the SRS data are the richer public source. Composition questions (what is the waste made of) use the SRNL batch measurements for SRS and the BBI chemicals for Hanford, accepting that one is a process-stream view and the other a tank-average view. Machine-learning work that ignores this mapping will learn the basis differences, not the waste differences."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "4.2 Why the activity densities differ"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The 2.5-fold activity-density difference (5.8 versus 2.3 MCi/Mgal) has three identifiable drivers. First, decay: Hanford's waste is older. The bulk of Hanford's curies were generated in the 1950s through 1970s and the BBI decays most tanks to 2022; SRS reprocessed fuel through the late 1980s and its inventory is correspondingly younger per curie of Cs-137 and Sr-90. But decay alone cannot produce a factor of 2.5 in 10 to 30 years for 30-year half-lives; the larger drivers are structural. Second, cesium management: SRS evaporated supernate to saltcake and kept the cesium in the tank system, with the salt phase carrying 45 percent of site activity in 91 percent of volume; Hanford's cesium left the tank system in ways SRS's did not, through documented single-shell tank leaks and the B-Plant cesium-recovery campaigns of the 1970s, leaving proportionally less cesium per remaining gallon. Third, strontium partitioning: Hanford's sludge fraction is larger and holds 91 percent of its Sr-90, while SRS's sludge volume is small (8 percent) so its Sr-90 concentrates in fewer gallons; per-gallon sludge activities at the two sites are actually similar (SRS Sludge Batch 10 measures 5,870 uCi/g dried solids Sr-90), and the density difference comes mostly from how much sludge each site retains per gallon of total waste."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "A practical implication follows for vitrification planning. The waste that dominates SRS's remaining mission is a well-characterized, high-cesium brine and saltcake stream that the site already knows how to decontaminate (SWPF) and grout (Saltstone). The waste that dominates Hanford's remaining mission is a strontium- and actinide-bearing sludge inventory spread across 149 aging single-shell tanks plus 28 DSTs whose supernatants await treatment. The sites' feed-composition problems differ accordingly: SRS's binding constraints are mercury control in DWPF offgas and I-129 and Tc-99 management in the salt system, while Hanford's are aluminum dissolution, chromium in LAW glass, and the sheer tank count for retrieval."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "4.3 What the batch measurements add"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The SRNL qualification data are the only public SRS numbers that measure current waste composition analyte by analyte, and they carry three observations that neither the closure tables nor the farm totals provide. First, the SRS sludge system is a stable, well-mixed composition: Sludge Batch 11 elementals (16,600 ug/g Fe, 15,500 ug/g Al, 6,010 ug/g U, 4,710 ug/g Hg) are consistent batch to batch, meaning DWPF glass models can be trained on a tight composition manifold rather than a wide one. Second, the SRS salt system is running close to its WAC envelope on the long-lived mobile nuclides: Salt Batch 14 measured 1.15e5 pCi/mL Tc-99 (54 percent of WAC) and 103 pCi/mL I-129, slightly above the 100 pCi/mL limit, and SRNL notes batches 11 and 12 also exceeded the I-129 limit; this is a live process-control signal, not a hypothetical. Third, actinide decontamination in the salt path is deep: batch Pu-241 at 0.6 percent of WAC and Cm-244 at 0.02 percent, confirming that the SWPF decontamination factor keeps the grout feed actinides far below regulatory concern and that the salt system's radiological risk is concentrated in Cs-137, Tc-99, and I-129."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "4.4 Data-model implications for machine learning"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "For ML work the two datasets have complementary strengths. The Hanford BBI is dense and current (177 tanks, 50 nuclides, 140 chemicals, phase-resolved) but has no closure-residual analogue, so models trained on it learn operational-state features. The SRS closure matrix is smaller (51 tanks, 54 nuclides, 27 chemicals) but is the only public dataset with a full per-tank closure-residual field, and it comes with ground truth for three tanks (the measured actuals), which is exactly the held-out-validation structure an ML pipeline needs: train on assigned values, validate against measured residuals. The batch-qualification table (607 rows, 14 samples, analyte-by-matrix) provides the measured-composition anchor for the SRS side. A concrete workflow that respects the bases: predict closure residuals from tank attributes using the SRS matrix; predict current phase compositions from BBI features on the Hanford side; and use the SRNL measurements as the transfer-learning bridge between SRS closure chemistry and current chemistry, since the same analytes (Fe, Al, Hg, U, NO3, NO2) appear in both."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Two cautions from this assembly effort itself. The ND left-shift defect inflated SRS closure totals by 10 percent and would have produced a confidently wrong paper had validation against the source tables' own totals not caught it; any pipeline consuming wide-format PDF tables should assert row sums against printed totals as a matter of course. And the Hanford BBI's per-tank effective dates mean site-level aggregates are sums of snapshots, not a snapshot of sums; analyses sensitive to time (decay, retrieval progress) must carry the per-tank date vector, which the database retains on every row."},
]

CONCLUSIONS = [
    {"t": "h1", "text": "5. Conclusions"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "This paper assembled the first unified, per-analyte database of the two largest remaining U.S. tank-waste inventories and used it to compare the Savannah River and Hanford tank farms on a like-for-like basis where one exists and on documented bases where it does not. The findings: SRS holds 33.2 Mgal and 191 MCi (September 2025), 63 percent of Hanford's 53.0 Mgal but 156 percent of its 122.5 MCi, a 2.5-fold activity-density gap driven by cesium retention in SRS salt waste, Hanford's older decayed inventory, and strontium partitioning into Hanford's larger sludge fraction. Sludge holds 55 percent of activity at both sites, but SRS carries nearly half its activity in mobile liquid phases against Hanford's quarter. Hanford's inventory is heavy-tailed (eight tanks hold 38 percent of activity) while SRS's assigned-closure distribution is deliberately flat. Chemically, Hanford is a 150,200-tonne sodium-nitrate-nitrite system with a 566 t chromium legacy; SRS's residual solids total 299 t dominated by Fe, Al, Hg, and U, and its live process risks are mercury in DWPF offgas and I-129 and Tc-99 near SWPF waste-acceptance limits."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The methodological finding is as important as the numeric one. The public data models of the two sites are structurally different: Hanford publishes a current per-tank Best-Basis Inventory, SRS publishes conservative closure residuals per tank plus farm-level current totals plus batch qualification measurements. A cross-site dataset that does not encode that difference, with dataset labels, basis descriptions, and per-row provenance, will silently mix bases and produce errors measured in orders of magnitude. The database released with this paper encodes the basis on every row, includes the validation record (22 of 22 FTF tanks reproduce their source-table totals; Tanks 18 and 19 assigned values equal their measured actuals), and exposes the SRS closure matrix with three-tank ground truth as the only public per-tank closure-residual dataset suitable for held-out validation of inventory-prediction models. The two inventories, read carefully, are complementary rather than parallel: one site's public data describe where it is going, and the other's describe where it must not end up."},
]

